-Over the last three years, large language models (LLMs) have entered agent-based simulation as decision engines. Generative agents endowed with natural-language reasoning, episodic memory and planning were introduced by Park et al. (2023). They have since been proposed for travel demand modelling (Liu et al., 2024), for large-scale urban simulation (Bougie & Watanabe, 2025), and for route replanning under road disruptions on the GAMA platform (Alves et al., 2026). The present work is the continuation of the software architecture of Vu, Gaudou & Oberoi (2025), which couples GAMA (Taillandier et al., 2019) with an OpenTripPlanner routing service and an LLM decision module, and applies it to the Toulouse metropolitan area.
+Over the last three years, large language models (LLMs) have entered agent-based simulation as decision engines. Generative agents endowed with natural-language reasoning, episodic memory and planning were introduced by Park et al. (2023). They have since been proposed for travel demand modelling (Liu et al., 2024), for large-scale urban simulation (Bougie & Watanabe, 2025), and for route replanning under road disruptions on the GAMA platform (Alves et al., 2026). The present work is the continuation of the software architecture of Vu, Gaudou & Oberoi (2025), which couples GAMA with an OpenTripPlanner routing service and an LLM decision module; we apply it to the Toulouse metropolitan area.
 
